package Lessons

use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

class TerrainGames is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/Scenes/terrainGames.html")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("Learn how a game car works as a physics simulation, set up a toy car in Quorum Studio, and drive it on a terrain.")

        Title("Driving on Terrain: Vehicles and Physics")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("Racing games, delivery games and open-world adventures all have one thing in common: something with
wheels moving over the ground. In this tutorial, you will add a toy car to a scene with a terrain,
set it up in Quorum Studio with a Vehicle controller, and drive it over the hills you made. Along the
way you will see how a game turns a car into physics: a body with weight, four wheels on springs, an
engine that pushes and brakes that hold, and tires that grip until they slide.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Explain how a game models a car: a body, wheels on springs, an engine, steering and brakes")
        ListItem("Set up a car in Quorum Studio and drive it with a short program")
        ListItem("Describe how mass, springs, grip and the width between the wheels change the way a car drives")
        ListItem("Change one setting at a time and describe what it does")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Think about riding in a car or on a bike over a bumpy road. What keeps you from feeling every single
bump? What makes a car skid on ice, or tip over on a sharp turn? Make a list of the parts of a real
car that matter for how it moves.")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/scenes/Terrain/Vehicles/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("New Code to Learn")
        Paragraph("You will be using the following new pieces of code:")
        Table("New Code to Learn", "media/scenes/Terrain/Vehicles/Code.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_PROGRAM_DEVELOPMENT_12)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_PROGRAM_DEVELOPMENT_13)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_DATA_INVESTIGATION_26)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_GAME_DEVELOPMENT_DESIGN_AND_DEVELOPMENT_3)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        Paragraph("A car in a game is a physics simulation, built from the same ideas as the falling ball in "):Link("Properties", "/tutorials/Scenes/properties.html"):Text(": mass, forces, gravity and friction. What makes a car special is how those ideas are put together.
A heavy body sits on four springs, each spring rests on a wheel, the engine pushes the wheels, the
brakes hold them, and the tires grip the ground until they are pushed too hard. Quorum has classes
for each of these parts. In this tutorial you will add a toy car to a scene, give it a Vehicle
controller in Quorum Studio, and tune how it drives with sliders. A short program then drives it.")
        HowAGameCarWorks()
        GettingTheCar()
        AddingTheCar()
        GivingTheCarAController()
        PhysicsAndGravity()
        WhereTheCameraFollowsFrom()
        TheProgram()
        Driving()
        HowTheSceneBuildsTheCar()
        WhyCarsTipAndSkid()
    end

    private action HowAGameCarWorks
        Subsection("How a game car works")
        Paragraph("A real car's wheels are round, and they roll. Simulating every wheel as a real round object,
touching the ground at just the right point and rolling without slipping, is hard for a physics
engine to do well, so many games use a shortcut called a "):Term("raycast vehicle"):Text(". Quorum's "):Code("Vehicle"):Text(" class is one.")
        Paragraph("In a raycast vehicle, the wheels aren't physics objects at all. Each wheel is an invisible line, a "):Term("raycast"):Text(", sent straight down from the car's body on every physics step. The line finds out how far away the
ground is under that corner of the car. A "):Term("spring"):Text(" then pushes the body up: the closer the ground, the more the spring is squeezed, and the harder it
pushes. This is the car's "):Term("suspension"):Text(", and it is why the body rides smoothly over bumps instead of bouncing off them. "):Term("Damping"):Text(" slows the spring down, the way a shock absorber does, so the car settles after a bump instead of
bouncing on and on.")
        Paragraph("The wheels also push the car along and turn it. The engine's force is applied where each driving
wheel touches the ground, the front wheels turn to steer, and each tire grips the ground sideways.
If a tire is pushed sideways harder than its grip, which Quorum calls its "):Term("friction slip"):Text(", it slides, and the car skids.")
        Paragraph("In Quorum, the car has two parts that move together. Its "):Term("physics body"):Text(" is an invisible box that the physics moves; the wheels' rays hang under it. The "):Strong("model"):Text(", the car you see, rides on the body. Because they are separate, the model can be any shape, and the
box can be smaller than it, so it doesn't catch on hills.")
    end

    private action GettingTheCar
        Subsection("Getting the car")
        Paragraph("The car is the "):Strong("Toy Car"):Text(", one of the free sample models the Khronos Group shares so that people can test 3D programs. The
Khronos Group is the same group that looks after OpenGL, WebGL and Vulkan, and they also created "):Strong("glTF"):Text(", the file format the car is saved in. The car model was made by Guido Odendahl, and its materials
and scene by Eric Chadwick, and they released it under the "):Strong("CC0"):Text(" license, so anyone may use it in any project.")
        Paragraph("The car's files are on GitHub, in the "):Link("Toy Car folder of glTF-Sample-Assets", "https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF-Sample-Assets/tree/main/Models/ToyCar"):Text(". That folder holds the model in more than one form; this tutorial uses the one in its "):Strong("glTF"):Text(" folder, which is a "):Code(".gltf"):Text(" text file, a "):Code(".bin"):Text(" file of shapes, and the images that color the car.")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("In your project's folder, make a folder named "):Code("Car"):Text(" at the top of the project.")
        ListItem("Open the "):Link("glTF folder", "https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF-Sample-Assets/tree/main/Models/ToyCar/glTF"):Text(" in a web browser. It lists ten files:")
        NestedBulletList()
        ListItem():Code("ToyCar.gltf"):Text(" and "):Code("ToyCar.bin"):Text(";")
        ListItem():Code("ToyCar_basecolor.png"):Text(", "):Code("ToyCar_clearcoat.png"):Text(", "):Code("ToyCar_emissive.png"):Text(", "):Code("ToyCar_normal.png"):Text(" and "):Code("ToyCar_occlusion_roughness_metallic.png"):Text(";")
        ListItem():Code("Fabric_baseColor.png"):Text(", "):Code("Fabric_normal.png"):Text(" and "):Code("Fabric_occlusion.png"):Text(".")
        EndNestedList()
        ListItem("For each file, open it on GitHub and use its "):Strong("Download raw file"):Text(" button, then move the file into your "):Code("Car"):Text(" folder. All ten files must be in the same folder, because "):Code("ToyCar.gltf"):Text(" finds the others by name.")
        ListItem("Also download the folder's "):Code("LICENSE.md"):Text(", from the "):Link("Toy Car folder", "https://github.com/KhronosGroup/glTF-Sample-Assets/tree/main/Models/ToyCar"):Text(", into "):Code("Car"):Text(", so the license stays with the model.")
        Paragraph("Use a scene with a terrain, such as one you shaped and painted in the earlier tutorials, saved as "):Code("Scenes/Scene.qs"):Text(".")

        Subsubsection("Giving credit")
        Paragraph("The car is CC0, so credit isn't required, but it is good practice, as in "):Link("Terrain Materials and Painting", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrainMaterials.html"):Text(". Add the car to your project's "):Code("Credits.txt"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/TerrainGames/Credits.txt", "")
    end

    private action AddingTheCar
        Subsection("Adding the car to the scene")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("Open your scene with a terrain. In the palette, under "):Strong("Custom 3D"):Text(", choose "):Strong("Custom Model"):Text(", then choose "):Code("Car/ToyCar.gltf"):Text(". A tile named "):Strong("ToyCar"):Text(" is added to a new "):Strong("Project Models"):Text(" group at the top of the palette. The toy is only a few centimeters long, so its tile starts it many
times larger, as its description says. The scene keeps the tile, so it is there the next time you
open the scene.")
        ListItem("Drag the ToyCar tile onto the terrain, or press Enter on it, and place the car.")
        ListItem("In the Properties panel, set its "):Strong("Name"):Text(" to "):Code("Car"):Text(", and its "):Strong("Scale"):Text(" to "):Code("150"):Text(" for x, y and z, so it's about the size of a real car.")
        ListItem("The toy car sits on a blanket, which is its own part of the model. In the "):Strong("Parts"):Text(" card, uncheck "):Strong("Show Fabric"):Text(". The blanket is hidden, in the editor and in the game, and the model's file isn't changed.")
    end

    private action GivingTheCarAController
        Subsection("Giving the car a controller")
        Paragraph("Select the car. In the "):Strong("Controller"):Text(" section, choose "):Strong("Vehicle"):Text(". The car is fitted at once: a physics box, four wheels and their springs are worked out from the
shape of the parts that show. Lines drawn over the car show them, and the car is drawn see-through
while they show:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("a light blue box: the physics body, with an arrow out of its front;")
        ListItem("orange circles: the front wheels, which steer; yellow circles: the back wheels;")
        ListItem("green lines: the springs, as long as they are at rest;")
        ListItem("a pink cross: where the game's camera starts.")
        Paragraph("The same values are in the Controller section's sliders. The toy car's front points along its own
-Z, so if the arrow points out of the back of the car, choose "):Strong("-Z"):Text(" under "):Strong("Front faces"):Text(", and press "):Strong("Fit to model"):Text(" again.")
        Paragraph("The Controller section has six cards:")
        Table("Controller cards", "media/scenes/Terrain/Vehicles/ControllerCards.csv")
        Paragraph("Every change is one step to undo. The car's own "):Strong("Physics"):Text(" card is replaced by a note while it has a controller, since the controller sets its physics.")
    end

    private action PhysicsAndGravity
        Subsection("Physics and gravity")
        Paragraph("Select the 3D layer itself in the Scene tree. Its "):Strong("Physics"):Text(" section has "):Strong("Physics in games"):Text(", which turned on when you chose Vehicle, and "):Strong("Gravity x"):Text(", "):Strong("y"):Text(" and "):Strong("z"):Text(", which start at 0, -9.8 and 0: Earth's pull, straight down. The editor itself never runs physics;
these are for the game.")
    end

    private action WhereTheCameraFollowsFrom
        Subsection("Where the camera follows from")
        Paragraph("Choose "):Strong("Game Camera"):Text(" in the Scene tree. The view moves to where the game's camera will start, behind and above the car,
facing it. Move it with the camera keys or the mouse; it keeps facing the car, and when you stop,
its place becomes the car's "):Strong("Behind"):Text(", "):Strong("Above"):Text(" and "):Strong("Angle"):Text(".")
    end

    private action TheProgram
        Subsection("The program")
        Paragraph("Replace the code in "):Code("Main.quorum"):Text(" with this program:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/TerrainGames/Main.quorum")
        Paragraph("Run it. The car drops onto its springs where you placed it, and the camera follows from where you
set it.")
    end

    private action Driving
        Subsection("Driving")
        Table("Driving", "media/scenes/Terrain/Vehicles/DrivingKeys.csv")
        Paragraph("The car's driving behavior turns these keys into three commands: "):Term("throttle"):Text(", from full reverse to full forward; "):Term("steering"):Text(", from full left to full right; and "):Term("braking"):Text(". Every key can be changed. For example, to drive with the arrow keys, add "):Code("use Libraries.Interface.Behaviors.Games.VehicleMovementBehavior"):Text(" at the top, and these lines in "):Code("CreateGame"):Text(", after the car is found:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/scenes/TerrainGames/ArrowKeys.quorum")
    end

    private action HowTheSceneBuildsTheCar
        Subsection("How the scene builds the car")
        Paragraph("When the game loads the scene, the car's controller builds it, so the program only finds it and
drives it.")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("The body"):Text(" is a box, lower and shorter than the model, so its nose, tail and belly clear slopes. It weighs 800
kilograms, and its turn damping of 0.3 makes 30 percent of its turning speed fade each second.")
        ListItem():Strong("The model"):Text(" rides on the body, at the offset in the Model card, and has no physics of its own.")
        ListItem():Strong("Four wheels"):Text(" hang under the body, each a ray straight down with a spring. The springs sag under the car's weight
by 9.8 divided by 4 times the stiffness, whatever the car's mass: about 0.25 at a stiffness of 10.
The front two steer, and the engine turns the ones chosen in the Wheels card, the rear two at first.")
        ListItem():Strong("The camera"):Text(" follows the body from behind and above, if Camera follows this is checked.")
    end

    private action WhyCarsTipAndSkid
        Subsection("Why cars tip and skid")
        Paragraph("When a car turns, its tires pull it sideways, toward the inside of the turn. That pull happens at
the ground, but the car's weight is centered higher up, at its "):Term("center of mass"):Text(". The higher the center of mass, and the faster the turn, the more that sideways pull tries to roll
the car over its outside wheels. What holds it down is its weight, resting on those outside wheels.
The farther apart the wheels are, the harder the weight resists tipping.")
        Paragraph("The car's settings are tuned for this:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Wheels out"):Text(" sets how far out to each side the wheels are. A wider stance resists tipping.")
        ListItem():Strong("Roll influence"):Text(", 0, applies the tires' sideways pull at the height of the body's center instead of at the ground,
so turning doesn't roll the car at all. Real cars aren't that lucky; this is one of the places a
game makes driving easier than real life.")
        ListItem():Strong("Tire grip"):Text(", 3, is about three times a real tire's. The more grip, the more suddenly a slide comes when it does.")
        ListItem():Strong("Steering lock"):Text(", 0.35, keeps the front wheels from turning too sharply, and "):Strong("Steering speed"):Text(", 3, turns them in over a third of a second instead of at once.")
        ListItem():Strong("Turn damping"):Text(", 0.3: much more fights the tires in a turn.")
        Paragraph("Slopes test the body and the springs. A low, short box clears the start and end of a hill, and soft
springs let the wheels drop and stay on the ground over a crest. A raycast wheel can't reach past
its rest length, so it can drop only as far as the car's weight presses its spring.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you and a partner add the toy car to a scene with a terrain and drive it around.
The goal is to get a working vehicle running in your own scene and feel how the physics settings
change the way it drives. Use a scene with a terrain that has some hills and valleys, such as one you
made in "):Link("Shaping Terrain", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrainShape.html"):Text(" or "):Link("Generating Terrain", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrainGenerate.html"):Text(". Follow "):Quoted("Getting the car"):Text(", "):Quoted("Adding the car to the scene"):Text(" and "):Quoted("Giving the car a controller"):Text(", replace "):Code("Main.quorum"):Text(" with the program on this page, and run it. Take turns driving: one partner drives while the other
gives directions, such as "):Quoted("drive to the top of the nearest hill"):Text(", "):Quoted("go down into the valley and back up"):Text(", or "):Quoted("make a full circle without tipping over"):Text(". Press R whenever the car needs a fresh start.")
        Paragraph("When you've both had a turn, try changing one setting at a time in the Controller section, running
the game again after each change and writing down what you notice. For example:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("halve "):Strong("Engine force"):Text(", from 3000 to 1500: can the car still climb the same hill?")
        ListItem("set "):Strong("Wheels out"):Text(" to about two thirds of what it is, or "):Strong("Roll influence"):Text(" to 0.5: does the car tip more easily on sharp turns?")
        ListItem("set "):Strong("Tire grip"):Text(" to 1, then to 10: what happens to turning and skidding?")
        ListItem("double "):Strong("Stiffness"):Text(", from 10 to 20: does the ride over bumps change?")
        ListItem("in the layer's Physics section, set "):Strong("Gravity y"):Text(" from -9.8 to -1.6, the Moon's: how do hills and bumps change?")
        Paragraph("Undo each change before trying the next, so you know which change did what.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("A game car is a physics simulation built from parts: a heavy body, springs on invisible rays, an
engine, steering and tires with limited grip. Many of its numbers were chosen to make driving fun
rather than perfectly real, such as a roll influence of 0. Which of the settings you tried made the
car feel most realistic, and which made it most fun to drive? When would a game choose fun over
realism?")
    end
end
